{
  "_comment": "Named-account contract, pinned across lib/pure.js (GNOME), claude-code/accounts.js (CLI + MCP + status line) and ClaudeUsageCore/Accounts.swift (macOS). A profile is one saved Claude Code login: the credentials blob plus the oauthAccount block of ~/.claude.json. tokenState decides whether a stored login can be used as is (valid), needs its refresh token exchanged first (stale), or must be logged in again (expired). autoSwitch picks the account to move to when the active one crosses the threshold - the candidate with the most headroom, only if it sits at least `margin` points under the threshold, never twice within `cooldownMs` of the last switch. That last switch is store state, not process state: every port's switchTo writes <accounts dir>/.last-switch.json = {at: <epochMs>, from: <name or null>, to: <name>} and every autoSwitch caller reads `at` from it, so a switch made by the CLI, the MCP tool or the other panel counts. A switch in progress is store state too: switchTo writes <accounts dir>/.switch-pending.json = {at, from, to} before it touches the live login and removes it once both halves are in; syncBackPlan never snapshots while it stands (a torn login whose old token Claude Code rotated matches no profile by token), and clears it once the target's token and account block are both live. liveLogin: the live access token names the profile first, the account block second. sameName: names collide ignoring case (APFS). parkName: an unsaved login parked before a switch. keychain: the macOS Keychain items to read, Claude Code's per-config-dir suffix included; `sha256` maps each NFC hash input to its digest so a port without a hash library can still check the rule. keychainWrite: the `security -i` stdin line that stores the credentials, so the tokens never sit in argv where ps shows them; null means the write is refused, which a caller finds out before it touches the live login. `security -i` reads one command into a 4096-byte buffer, so a line of 4096 bytes or more (newline included) is refused; `repeat` repeats `secret` that many times and `expectedBytes` pins the UTF-8 length of the line instead of spelling it out. The four store decisions every port's I/O layer only carries out: saveRefusal (the live login may not be saved as `name`: a case variant, then a name holding another account unless `force`, then a twin, which `force` never overrules; null = save), refresh (the OAuth block after a refresh-token answer, from `refreshOauth` at `now`; `expires_in` moves expiresAt only as a finite number above zero, anything else keeps the old expiry; null = no access token, refused), torn (the account block names a saved profile other than `name`) and switchPlan (after syncBack: who the switch leaves, whether the unsaved live login is parked first, and stay / repair / expired / refresh / install). sameJSON: the syncBack no-op compare, key order ignored, scalars strict. usageCacheEntry: one row of <accounts dir>/.usage-cache.json.",
